I'll use Dijkstra with a heap keyed on `(cost, path)`. Because the heap orders entries by cost and then by the full path list, the first time we pop `dst` we already have the cheapest path with the lexicographically smallest node list among equal costs. Storing the path with each entry keeps the tie rule exact, at some memory cost, which is acceptable here. Nodes that appear only as edge targets are handled by `graph.get(node, [])`.

